  silent [38;5;153mundefined[39m at runtime. Untestable under Node without a DOM shim. Complexity: trivial.

  [1m2. Native ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m with
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m importing it. [3mBuys:[0m explicit dependencies, no globals, no load-order hazard; the modern default and the best
  long-term shape for a growing app. [3mCosts:[0m ES modules don't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need a dev server to open the
  page at all — a real workflow change for a repo with no tooling. Still can't be [38;5;153mrequire[39md by the CommonJS [38;5;153msrc/[39m side.
  Complexity: moderate.

  [1m3. Dual-format module[0m [3m(recommended)[0m — [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m is written as plain CommonJS and ends with a ~4-line footer that
  attaches [38;5;153mwindow.Tracking[39m when there's no [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m. Pages include it with a normal script tag exactly as in
  approach 1; Node tests [38;5;153mrequire()[39m it directly. [3mBuys:[0m the zero-build, [38;5;153mfile://[39m-friendly loading the repo already uses,
  [1mplus[0m the ID-lifecycle logic becomes testable under plain Node — which matters because the tricky parts here
  (first-visit generation, reload persistence, logout behavior, corrupt/absent storage) are pure logic worth pinning
  down, and you have no test runner today. [3mCosts:[0m the footer is mildly unidiomatic boilerplate, and it keeps the
  load-order requirement from approach 1. Complexity: moderate.

  I recommend [1m3[0m. Approach 2 is the better architecture in the abstract, but making a dev server mandatory to open a
  static login page is a steep tax on a repo with no build step, and it still leaves the logic untestable from [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
  Approach 3 gets the testability, which is the thing I'd least want to give up on identity code that has to be right
  across every form you add later.

  I considered and discarded adding a bundler (Vite/webpack): it solves module delivery properly but introduces a build
  step, a dependency tree, and config to a repo that currently has none — not justified by one shared module.
